From 3c78b84617454d308f9e81335f3338f40f3029e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 14:41:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/30] windows: map the external kernel with a section, and
 name the krunfw DLL

Three loose ends, and libkrun type-checks for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc after
them.

map_kernel() handled the raw-x86_64 kernel by mmap'ing it read-only and handing
the address to the VMM as a KernelBundle. CreateFileMappingW plus MapViewOfFile
is the same operation: file-backed, read-only, whole file, address chosen by
the OS, page-aligned, and outliving the handle it was made from. Both spellings
move behind map_file_readonly() so the two platforms differ only in the mapping
call. The mapping is never torn down on either side, which is what the VMM
requires -- the guest reads that memory for as long as it runs.

KRUNFW_NAME gains the versioned-DLL spelling. No libkrunfw is built for Windows
today, so the load will fail; that is not fatal, because the bundled kernel is
only consulted when the caller supplied neither an external kernel nor firmware,
and Windows callers pass a kernel path to krun_set_kernel(). Naming it keeps the
diagnostic legible instead of leaving a bare cfg hole.

krun_get_shutdown_eventfd() returns int32_t, which cannot carry a HANDLE.
krun_create_ctx() only builds a shutdown eventfd on macOS/aarch64, so the arm is
unreachable, but truncating a handle to fit would hand back a plausible-looking
bogus value -- return -ENOTSUP instead.
